[Music]  thank you everybody for attending our  session here today my name is Chris  Latimer I'm one of the product managers  for Google that focuses on api's and API  management before we start the talk  today I'm curious have any of you or how  many of you have been in a session where  we've done questions and answer is using  dory ok so it's new to a bunch of you so  this is this is a internal Google tool  that we use all the time for big  sessions like this where we want to make  sure that not only you have a chance to  ask your questions but also that from an  audience perspective you can vote up and  vote down questions so we can make sure  that we're addressing and answering the  things that you feel are you know the  most important as a group and of course  we'll have mics here and over here as  well so when we get to the Q&amp;A portion  of the talk feel free to step up to the  microphone and we'll be happy to answer  any questions that you may have so a  little bit of background on on me so I  joined Google about two years ago before  that I was working for a company called  apogee a lot of you may have heard of  apogee we're an api management platform  we got bought by Google and so for the  last four or so years I've been spending  almost all my time talking with  customers different companies about the  API programs that they're creating the  challenges they're running into you  trying to figure out how it is that we  can build a platform that's going to  make our customers that the most  successful as we can and before that I  was interested in api's because I was  building a lot of api's and when I when  I first started getting into API there  was a big debate sort of raging in  industry between things like should we  use soap or should we use rest and at  the time it seemed like soap it  basically won enterprises were viewing  api's as this sort of a novelty  technology for the Facebook's and  Twitter's of the world and not really  something that serious enterprises would  need or be rebuilding to satisfy their  use cases and then a couple things  happen we had things like big  improvements in the way that websites  worked so we went from pages that needed  to  every time two single page apps that are  much more responsive and if you look at  frameworks like angular and others that  have come out they natively consume this  JSON and so all of a sudden we had  enterprises building api's to support  these these new types of websites and  then of course at the same time mobile  just became you know it's the explosion  that became and as a result the same  type of thing applied so if you've ever  tried to consume a soap service from say  like an iOS app or build an iOS app that  uses soap it's a pretty challenging  exercise it's pretty trivial if you're  using a restful api and so we had a lot  of companies that we're starting to  build more and more and more api's and  so it's great that today we're sitting  here and in the sort of this you know  completely filled room talking about api  is and somewhat surprising that so even  though it was sort of the de facto  winner is you know i'm i'm pretty  surprised if we find a soap session how  to build better soap services on App  Engine or how to optimize your  kubernetes cluster for soap but we've  got a lot of people that are interested  in an API management so a lot of you are  probably like why are you talking about  the history of API so what's this has to  do with anything I came to a best  practices talk so the reason why I like  to start this talk by sort of describing  a little historical context is that the  circumstances that that sort of happened  around web and mobile led to a bunch of  API programs sort of springing up just  out of necessity without a lot of  foresight and this gives us the ability  to go and sort of look at a lot of  common problems that have happened from  these programs which really had no no  management upfront and so one of the  things that I the patterns that I  observed as I go and talk to a lot of  enterprise customers who've built out a  lot of api's is that they don't really  set out to build an API program usually  what happens is something more like this  where a project just happens to come  along maybe we're gonna build out  something an angular if somebody says  let's build a new you know customer  facing web site let's build something  for our call center great we're gonna do  that and it just so happens that this  application needs an API we launched it  everything is going according to plan so  far so good until a whole  other projects come along in each one of  those projects needs to create api's as  well so we end up with mobile apps we  end up with cell service kiosk we end up  with all these things that are needing  api's and so then we start to run into  some of the challenges and we start to  see the the need for API management so  if you start looking across these api's  you'll see that they're really not  necessarily consistent you'll see things  like maybe I've got a preferred approach  for doing things like pagination maybe I  like to use limited offset maybe you  like to use page size and you know page  number or maybe somebody else likes to  use a current set of records maybe I  like to use customer uppercase a  uppercase D and you like customer  uppercase a lowercase D or camel cases  or you know all these different sort of  permutations and so what ends up  happening is that as a developer as you  go to try to use these api's you're sort  of having to learn each one over and  over again we sort of have more friction  than we need to have as we're trying to  build API driven applications in our  organization the other thing that  happens is we tend to run into a lot of  the same type of challenges so if you  have ten different teams that are  building api's you may have 10 different  teams that are building out solutions  for things like caching like traffic  management quotas security and so forth  and so not only are these API is sort of  inconsistent but we're spending a lot  more effort than we need to to actually  get them up and running and out the door  and then probably worst of all is that  because these api's are sort of hiding  in silos within your organization they  can be hard to find and so we end up  with sort of API is that that ultimately  do their purpose but we don't get a lot  of the reusability aspects out of them  we don't get a lot of the streamlined  benefits and people aren't able to find  them and usually it's about this time  when companies recognize but we should  get more serious about API management  and how we're going to manage our API s  and so that's when they they typically  start to look at platforms like Apogee  and start to think you know what exactly  is it that we should be evaluating and  what sort of capabilities should we have  in an API management platform  so let's talk a little bit about that so  if you are currently you know you don't  have an API management platform you've  got something homegrown or you're just  sort of evaluating what sorts of  capabilities should we have to be able  to build an effective API program you'll  start to see things like this so we want  to have an API catalog so discovery is  important we want people to be able to  find api's we want people to be able to  use those api's and we want to start  thinking about things like the different  audiences that we might have for those  api's so we want to make sure our  platform can do things like support  internal api's and partner api's maybe  when somebody signs in we want to know  which audience they're in and we want to  be able to make sure that we can put in  place role back role based access  control to ensure that only the right  people should have access to those api's  depending on the types of things that  you're building you might want to have  cell service onboarding so I know in in  companies that I worked for a lot of  times the hardest part of her use was  just being able to track down the API  and talk to the right team to actually  get access to those one of the things  that API management can do for you is it  can streamline that entire process so we  can find the API is they can give you a  way to get credentials the same way you  would expect to be able to go to  Facebook or Twitter sign up for their  API and start using it right away  there's no reason why you shouldn't be  able to do that within your organization  as well and of course you should have  good documentation so this can of course  be things like if you're using open API  for documenting or specifying your API  structure we want to be able to sort of  hold that in but you may want to have  additional things as well you might want  to have sort of longer form  documentation that describes here's how  you get a lot open in our API program or  here's how we expect you to secure this  API maybe you want to put things in  place like SDKs or additional files that  become people can download these are all  things that you can start to think about  as you are looking at the capabilities  that you want from an API the portal  type experience another thing you want  to think about is analytics and sort of  how you're going to track things what  you need to track in the in your API so  of course you're gonna have things like  you probably want to know if all of us  you see a big uptick in errors that are  coming through or a big drop-off or  spike in traffic if things start taking  longer than you expected those are all  things that you want to be able to put  in place and you probably want to be  able to measure business impact to your  api's as well so an Apogee we like to  talk a lot about api's as a product and  taking your api's and productizing those  so that you can expose those to certain  certain audiences these can be things  that are internal maybe you've got  products that sort of represent a line  of business and you want to be able to  offer those capabilities to other lines  of business within your organization or  you might want to productize for an  external audience maybe you built things  internally but they've got some  sensitive information you want to create  something that's more partner specific  you don't necessarily want to go rewrite  that API from the ground up we want to  be able to look at things like product  ization to help with that type of  problem and you may even want to tie  that to monetization so a lot of apple  juice customers actually have taken  their API the data that the data that  they've gained over time and turn those  into monetized products that they offer  to the public or to specific business  partners so that's from a sort of a  consumption standpoint but we still want  to have all the capabilities that we  think about typically with an api  gateway so for example we want to have  in place access control now you might be  looking at things like ooofff as a  standard for API security but you may  need to tie into existing systems in  your your organization currently maybe  you use you know ping for identity  access management maybe you use you know  LDAP or Active Directory other products  that are out there you want to make sure  that you can sort of bridge that gap  between API security and the things that  you have to work with in your  organization likewise you want to think  about things like transformation and  mediation so these are capabilities  where you know perhaps if we have all  those inconsistent api's that people  have built and we don't want to go  rewrite them from the ground up you can  put an API management platform in place  and maybe we can have modernize some of  those query parameters that people have  built we can offer the same uniform  experience to developers without  actually going and rewriting everything  from the ground up  you probably wanna start thinking about  things like traffic management and  quotas so I know I  I did not have a sufficient appreciation  for how complex quotas could be when I  when I joined Apogee it actually seemed  like a pretty simple problem it was I  give you you know ten calls and I'm  gonna increment a counter so it gets to  ten and at eleven I'm gonna stop giving  you access what's so hard about that  and then I a was introduced to extremely  high volumes of API traffic and so if  you start getting things like like five  ten fifteen thousand requests per second  keeping a quota in sync and become a  challenge and then if that quote is  distributed across multiple geographies  it becomes a really non-trivial problem  so these are some of the things you want  to think about as you're looking at at  these gateway type capabilities an API  management of course we have to think  about threat protection anytime that we  go and we want to expose our data out to  the internet especially you're going to  want to make sure that you're thinking  about things like are we protecting  against you know DDoS attacks are we  protecting against people trying to put  in place equal injection cross-site  scripting those type of attacks so do we  have the capability in our platform to  be able to address those and then things  like caching and optimization so a lot  of times we built services that maybe  have been around for a long time back  before we had sort of you know a lot of  mobile use cases where customers  expected a very quick response time so  are there opportunities for us to build  and use caching in our platform to  improve that overall performance and  experience that we can deliver through  API driven apps and so as you go through  sort of this type of an exercise I'm  sure many of you have gone through this  type of an exercise typically you end up  making a selection you decide what  platform is going to work best for you  based on your use cases and it's about  that time that you kind of realize now  you almost have two problems because you  still have all of the API management  concerns that that you know you have  from all the api's that you've built and  all this type of problems and then you  have this new platform and you have to  figure out how exactly we're going to  configure this to build a successful API  API program and so  just like managing anything else when we  go to manage API is we really have to  ask ourselves you know what is what a  success will look like and how are we  going to track and how we're going to  measure success over time to make sure  we're on target to achieve our goals so  in talking with a lot of customers  I've asked I've asked so many customers  you know what exactly do you envision  when you think of an API program that's  successful how would you measure that  how would you think about that and I've  heard of a number of sort of common  themes over time and I've tried to  categorize these so the one of the first  questions that that new customers want  to ask is you know where should we gets  it where should we start and we've we  built a bunch of API should we just sort  of take every API every service we've  built dump it out on our dev portal let  people start using that and that's one  approach it's usually not the most  successful approach where I recommend  that customers start is by looking at  the end user and what's going to provide  value to those end users so I'll give  you an example so I worked with a large  retailer and so one of the first API is  that they they started with that they  really started to publish and and market  was a loyalty program and so we talked  about how they were measuring the value  like how do you know if you're actually  reaching these these end users and so  they did a couple things so one is they  were started to look at traffic for the  loyalty program how many people were  actually coming into their store because  they had loyalty points and redeeming  those points they started to look you  know they got some early signals by API  traffic so as they rolled this out to  partners and directly to consumers they  started to see yep we're actually  getting a pretty good uptick in terms of  people that are taking advantage of our  loyalty program and it's driving those  customers back to the store more and  they can sort of watch that usage you  know in in real time on a day to day  basis the the next aspect once you sort  of have identified that these api's are  going to allow us to provide value that  end user is to convince developers to  start using those api's now for an  internal program that's not necessarily  hard because we've got a little bit more  control over what our internal  developers are building on a day to day  basis  but as you're looking  sort of like public facing API our  partner API is very recruiting people  you want to make sure that you are  offering something to those application  developers and that could be in the form  of a you know better experience new  capabilities and their apps it could be  in the form of a you know direct you  know monetization channel we've got  retailers that are doing things like  revenue sharing monetization so when you  sign up for their API you can actually  drive your monetization of your  application and then when we look at how  customers are actually measuring this  one of the most common is actually  looking at the number of consuming apps  so if we've been able to reach the  audience that we're after we get people  to be aware of those API so they  actually starting to use those and build  those have we built an API that's that's  usable by developers and offer  sufficient value we'll also see the  number of developers that are using  those api's go up substantially and we  can also watch and see how our community  is growing you know do we see more  questions through either directly on our  portal or we're using things like Stack  Overflow we see more interest that are  climbing up and we can measure these and  we can take appropriate steps to you  know continue to encourage that type of  growth the third thing is that the API  is as they're being used they generate  some type of ROI for your organization  and this could be in a number of  different forms I mean this could be  from enabling more partners to onboard  on to your API sort of growing the  partner channels and the number of  people who can sell your products or use  your data it can be in the form for  especially for internal programs of just  greater internal efficiency there's a  lot of time that's wasted by people  trying to figure out there's a certain  API exist what team do I need to talk to  to get access to an API once I determine  that it does exist is there a way that  we can sort of drive efficiency from  that aspect also from the aspect of you  know as people are building those api's  how can we make our applications get  built faster and then we can also of  course sometimes just take the api's  directly and monetize those so we've got  customers out there like a large you  know whether site they'll offer you you  know subscription basis for getting  access to their weather data for example  others that have market data  or accessing things like you know credit  card processing so there are ways that  we can also look at these api's as a  direct new channel of business for our  organizations so when we kind of look  across you know the end to end going  from that end user that's using the  application making sure that we're  driving the ability for the developers  to get those apps up and running more  quickly to use those api's and to ensure  that we're driving the overall ROI we  can think of this as sort of happening  in two parts so the first part is API  exposure so what can we do to make the  ability for API teams to create API is  very quickly more efficiently to be able  to derive the the the proliferation of  API is within your organization and then  we can think about API consumption so  what are we doing once those api's are  up and running to actually make it easy  for people to get access to api's and  start building those things so we can  actually deliver the real tangible value  to our end-users and so again one of the  things that that some of the research we  did leading into this talk was to go and  ask customers you know what are the what  are the actual KPIs that you have found  that you're tracking how do you measure  the value of our API program and so when  it comes to the exposure size sort of  taking all the data and things that  you've built and getting an API out more  quickly we've heard two things so the  first is speed to API so we we've  recognized that need for something and  we want to get it up and running and  available and into the hands of  developers as quickly as possible and we  don't necessarily want to do that just  by throwing more people at the problem  and so we also want to make sure that  the overall cost in order to get that  API out the door is as small as possible  and sort of the key behind this is you  know we're an API management platform  can help is that we can eliminate a lot  of those the the work that you would  have to do otherwise so how can we use  an API platform to sort of allow us to  focus in just on the things that we care  about we don't necessarily want to be  experts of API management we want to be  experts of our API s and  and building the the underlying business  logic and so we can use an API  management platform for things like  security so using API management can we  eliminate the need for individual  security reviews every time an API gets  published can we do things like embed  all those standards that we have and our  standard controls and policies so that  anytime an API call comes in we're going  to automatically apply the the right  policies can we use it to tie in to our  existing IDPs and our existing  capability so that we don't have to sort  of reinvent the wheel every time things  like traffic management so you know we  start to think about some of the threats  that can happen even with data that's  not necessarily sensitive if we have to  be aware that even non sensitive data  can be important so for example we may  have like retailers that have store  hours not a sensitive bit of information  but it's something that's critical for  customers that want to go and look and  see if the store is open is we don't  necessarily want to open that up to the  public have a huge wave of traffic sort  of flow in and take take that capability  down we can use traffic management in  the API management platform to be able  to solve those type of use cases and  protect against those type of threats  again caching will come into play so can  we do things like not just response  caching at the edge but can we also do  things like partial caching maybe you're  built maybe you look at how your api's  are structured and you see that it would  actually work better instead of what you  have today if you aggregate two or three  API is maybe you see from analytics  there are patterns that are happening  over and over again you want to optimize  those api's but you're scared about  handing back stale data so what we might  want to do is we don't want to say well  we're gonna create sort of a mash-up  we're gonna grab two or three different  api's and put those payloads together  two of those things are cacheable we can  use partial caching and one's not and we  can still improve the overall response  time and performance of that API and  then likewise we get to things like like  transformation and mediation so if you  have an API that was built for maybe a  client-server app or something that's  running internally to your organization  and you want that to be useful for a  mobile app you can do things like mobile  optimization we can cut the payload down  sort of trim out the parts that aren't  needed by the mobile app  same thing for like data scrubbing so if  you've got an existing API it's got  sensitive customer information in it and  you want to expose that to a partner  instead of building another version of  that API you can use the mediation and  transformation capabilities scrub that  out so that your same API becomes  applicable and usable by more people and  then of course we want to be able to  track and get real-time visibility into  our API is if all of a sudden we see  that things are going are going bad  either errors are coming up or the  response time is starting to really  trail off we want to be able to get  visibility into that so that we can take  the appropriate action from an API  consumption standpoint some of the  things that we heard when we asked  customers were time to hello world so we  built an API portal we've let people  come become aware of our API s that we  built they can get access to those how  long does it actually take for them to  start building something with it even  just a simple hello world so how long  before we see they go from a registered  user to somebody that actually has an  app that's been sending traffic in so we  want to be able to monitor and track  that that data over time and then  improve our documentation and our  outreach so that we can improve those we  can also look at things like number of  developers so if we're trying to drive  consumption the consumption comes from  developers that are actually building  apps that are going to be using these  API s so we want to be able to see are  we actually getting the adoption that we  want are we reaching the audience of  developers that that we're after and  then are they actually building apps do  we see an uptick in terms of the number  of apps that are being built as we as we  launch more api's and so how do we do  that how can a how can  API management help with that well an  API management program should be  building a very compelling developer  experience for your developers you want  them to not only see here's the  documentation the api's that are  available but also see what types of  things are possible  we've got a lot of customers that are  building things like application  showcases so it's very easy for you to  go and say oh here's a neat app that  somebody built hopefully that triggers  ideas from other developers so that they  can go you know build their ideas with  the portal and you want to make it very  easy for  people to not just sort of see what  api's exists but actually try those out  as well so they can start using those  and they can start experimenting with  the api's and seeing how they work as  opposed to you know writing a little  test harness or things like that we can  actually do that right within the  browser with interactive Doc's you can  take an open API spec load it in create  interactive documentation and start  using that really right away and if we  are able to do this successfully we've  seen some major major improvements from  from our customers so here's a good  example from from Walgreens so they've  been a customer for a really long time  so what they started out with they  actually started out with a an API  called the quick prints API so somebody  at Walgreens recognized that you know we  have all of these photo printers that  are sitting in our stores and everybody  some of you right now are taking photos  with your phones and a lot of times they  just kind of sort of stay on the phone  people aren't printing them off anymore  so what could Walgreens do in order to  make it easy for people to take those  photos send them into the store and  their answer to that question was we'll  build a quick prints API so we'll build  an API we'll put it on our mobile app  but that'll let people take those  pictures send it to the store hopefully  we'll get a little trickle of revenue  that comes in and sort of justifies you  know this space in our store and what  they found is they didn't get a little  trickle of revenue they got a tremendous  boost in terms of those users so the  users that were actually entered in  interacting with Walgreens on their  mobile channel on their store channel  the brick-and-mortar and online we're  spending about 6x  well what anybody else was the folks  that would just come into the store  because these customers were coming in  to pick up their photo and then they  were buying shampoo they're writing  toothpaste or buying whatever else that  they needed at Walgreens and it ended up  being sort of a real eye-opener for them  once they realized that they said we  should open this up we should take this  API and we should let anybody use our  quick prints API and so they started to  offer this to lots of other mobile apps  that are out there that allow you to  take photos and print photos off and so  now they're up to about 300 partner  that are building api's on top of this  quick prince and that's 300 partners  that are constantly sending people into  the stores to come in to pick up those  photos and buy other things at Walgreens  and it's just made a tremendous impact  and so when I think of the successful  API program  I view Walgreens as really the archetype  that I feel like a lot of companies  should strive for because it's not just  necessarily an IT problem it's really  something that can deliver tremendous  overall business value  [Music]

